package cosmonic:agent@0.4.4;

/// Start child agent sessions and work with them: send them input, wait for
/// their results, cancel them, end them, and see the tree they form.
/// Unstable behind `agent-spawn`.
///
/// A child is an agent session of its own: an agent component (targeting
/// `runnable-agent`) in its own workload, with its own conversation, grants,
/// toolsets and session state, inheriting nothing. Loading a toolset into
/// the caller's own workload is `tool-loading`, a separate power. Both share
/// the types and ceiling rules in `spawn-types`.
///
/// Tree. Every child session records its parent, so sessions form a tree
/// rooted at the session a person or trigger started. The caller lists its
/// descendants (state only) and acts only on its direct children, by session
/// id. A host shows the same tree from its session store and routes a
/// child's approvals to the root session's person, labelled with the
/// child's place in the tree.
///
/// Location and authority. Every operation names a child by session id and
/// works through the host's session store, so a child may run on another
/// host, host group or zone, and move between runs. A session id authorizes
/// nothing: the host records the caller identity (see `spawn-types`) that
/// spawned each child and acts only for it. When that identity ceases to
/// exist (its workload is deleted or recreated), its children end.
///
/// Runs and leases. Every run holds an exclusive lease in the session store
/// with a fencing generation: root runs, runs a parent starts, and runs a
/// person starts by talking to a child alike. The store must be linearizable
/// (compare-and-swap) for leases, generations, spawn tickets and limit
/// reservations; a store or zone that cannot be answers `unavailable`.
///
/// - The generation opens when the store grants the lease and closes when
///   the run ends for any reason or the lease expires (the operator's
///   timeout, recommended 60 seconds after the last renewal). The store
///   rejects any later write, outcome or dispatch it mediates under a closed
///   generation. Whichever host next reads a child whose lease expired
///   reports the run failed: waiters get `run-error.host-lost`, its state
///   becomes `failed`, and `child.failure` says why.
/// - The running host fences itself: once its monotonic clock shows the
///   store's timeout minus a margin (recommended 15 seconds) since it sent
///   its last renewal that succeeded, it stops the run's instances (agent and
///   toolsets), so nothing new starts through any import, and cancels what
///   is in flight. A renewal reply that arrives after the fence fired does
///   not revive the run.
/// - Every call the host brokers for a run (model calls, credential-brokered
///   egress, tool dispatch, writes through a host-managed store) carries the
///   generation. A broker on the run's host admits it only while that host's
///   own fence has not fired; a broker elsewhere checks the generation with
///   the store and fails closed when it cannot confirm it is open. Direct
///   egress through a component's own grants cannot be fenced.
/// - After a lease expires, the child's next run is accepted but starts only
///   once the operator's re-placement delay (at least the self-fencing
///   margin, recommended 30 seconds, counted from expiry) has passed; its
///   progress shows `blocked(placement-delay)`, and it holds its
///   `concurrent-agents` place and counts toward `max-duration` meanwhile.
/// - Fencing does not undo effects already under way, and cannot help a host
///   whose clock misbehaves (a paused virtual machine). Those may overlap a
///   new run, within the child's own reach.
///
/// After a run that did not finish, the store closes its generation and,
/// while no run holds the session, completes the journal with the kinds
/// `session` already reserves: an `agent.op-result.v1` with outcome
/// `unresolved` for each tool op that has no result (its effects are unknown,
/// not failed), then an `agent.run-result.v1` with outcome `cancelled` or
/// `failed` and an error naming why (cancelled, host lost, revoked). The
/// child's conversation is every step recorded under the closed generation,
/// then those entries. A harness resuming from it answers each unresolved
/// call to the model as a tool result saying the outcome is unknown and must
/// not be repeated blindly, so the history stays well-formed for model
/// providers. Nothing is replayed.
///
/// Stopping. `cancel` cancels a child's run in progress and every run in
/// progress below it, including runs a person started there; `end` also
/// ends the child and every session below it; a person's stop on a session
/// in the host's UI cancels its subtree the same way. A run that ends any
/// other way leaves its children's runs going.
///
/// - Barrier. Each first marks the whole subtree as cancelling in the
///   session store, atomically. Until the stop completes, every run
///   acceptance in the subtree is refused, whatever its path: `spawn` or
///   `send` from a run in it (`denied`, `start-error.denied`), `send` into it
///   from outside (`busy`), and runs a person or a trigger would start
///   through the host. The mark lifts when the stop completes.
/// - Force. Cancellation is cooperative first. After the operator's grace
///   period (recommended 15 seconds), each host still running a run in the
///   subtree stops its instances as it would to fence itself, and metering
///   stops; from then on the store refuses that run's lease renewals and
///   tells the renewing host to stop. A host the request cannot reach is
///   stopped by its lease: the run ends `host-lost`.
/// - Completion. `cancel` and `end` resolve when every run in the subtree has
///   ended or lost its lease: within the grace period plus the lease
///   timeout. Dropping the future only stops waiting. Effects already under
///   way are not undone.
///
/// Lifetime. A child lives until `end`, its `max-duration`, or its parent
/// session's end; ending a session ends its descendants. A child with no
/// run in progress holds no instance. An ended child stays listed for the
/// host's retention (recommended the 100 most recent per tree), and while it
/// does, `wait` still answers for its runs and `cancel` and `end` on it are
/// ok.
///
/// Limits. Always set, operator-adjustable, per tree: `depth` (default 3),
/// `concurrent-agents` (runs from acceptance until they end, default 20),
/// `live-sessions` (children not yet ended, default 50) and `total-agents`
/// (children spawned in all, default 500). A child's own subtree is further
/// held to its `budget.max-spawns`, which limits spawning only, never runs.
/// Every spawn and run acceptance reserves its place atomically.
///
/// Budgets. Every root session has a finite token and active-time budget,
/// which an operator may raise but never remove. Tokens count every model
/// call in the tree, the root's own included; active time is time during
/// which any run in the tree is in progress. A child's `budget` applies on
/// top, and a subtree's spend counts against the child and every ancestor.
/// The broker that serves model calls meters them: before each call it
/// reserves the estimated prompt plus the call's maximum output, refusing
/// the call when any budget on the path cannot cover it, and settles the
/// real usage afterwards, recorded by the host rather than under the run's
/// generation so a fenced run's spend still counts. A provider that reports
/// no usage is charged the estimate. When a budget is spent, runs below it
/// finish `max-total-tokens` or `max-duration` and no run is accepted below
/// it (`exhausted`) until an operator or person raises it.
///
/// Attachments belong to their session. On `send` (and `spawn` with input)
/// the host copies each stored attachment the parent can read into the
/// child's session and re-issues it. An outcome's stored attachments are
/// copied back the same way and count toward `output.max-bytes`; with
/// `output.structured-only` an outcome carries none.
///
/// Traces. The caller passes its own trace context as `trace-parent` (a W3C
/// `traceparent`; never `tracestate` or baggage). The implementer starts each
/// child run in that trace, as a child span while the caller's run is in
/// progress and as a linked span after it, and records spawn, send, cancel,
/// end, lease expiry and revocation as span events, with the session id as
/// the conversation id.
@unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
interface spawn {
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use inference-types.{user-message};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use run-types.{run-outcome};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use session-status.{status};
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use spawn-types.{
        spawn-request, admission-error, start-error, run-error, started-run,
        budget-item,
    };
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    use wasi:clocks/types@0.3.0.{duration};

    /// Why a child's last run failed, or why it takes no more runs.
    /// Implementer-authored.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-failure {
        /// The run's lease expired. The detail is an operator-named host
        /// group or zone, never a host name.
        host-lost(string),
        /// The child's components could not start.
        start-failed(string),
        /// A budget item is spent, its own or an ancestor's; no more runs
        /// until an operator or person raises it.
        exhausted(budget-item),
        /// Its admission no longer fits its ancestors' current ceilings or
        /// policy, so it takes no more runs.
        revoked(string),
        /// A reason not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// One session in the caller's subtree.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record child {
        /// Host-minted, unique, stable, and valid on any host that shares
        /// the session store.
        session: string,
        /// Its parent's session id.
        parent: string,
        /// A child of a root session is at depth 1.
        depth: u32,
        /// The request's labels for the caller's direct children; empty for
        /// deeper descendants, whose labels a child wrote.
        labels: list<tuple<string, string>>,
        /// Its `session-status`; none when the host could not read it.
        status: option<status>,
        /// Set when its last run failed or it takes no more runs.
        failure: option<child-failure>,
        /// The operator-named host group or zone it last ran in, for
        /// display; never a host name or an address.
        location: option<string>,
        /// What it and its subtree have spent, metered by the host.
        usage: usage-total,
        /// True once the session has ended.
        ended: bool,
    }

    /// Spend metered by the host for a session and its whole subtree.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record usage-total {
        /// Prompt plus completion tokens.
        tokens: u64,
        /// Runs accepted across the subtree (unlike `max-runs`, which counts
        /// one session's runs).
        runs: u32,
        /// Model calls made.
        model-calls: u32,
    }

    /// What the caller has left. Its own figures are exact; ancestor bounds
    /// are coarse (rounded down to a power of two tokens or a whole minute,
    /// and refreshed at most once a minute), so a session cannot read another
    /// subtree's spend through them.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record remaining-budget {
        /// Tokens left in the caller's own budget; none when its own budget
        /// sets no token limit.
        tokens: option<u64>,
        /// Time left under the caller's own limit, on that limit's own clock:
        /// wall-clock since spawn for a child's `max-duration`, active time
        /// for a root. None when it sets no limit.
        duration: option<duration>,
        /// Runs left from its own `max-runs`; none likewise.
        runs: option<u32>,
        /// The coarse token bound from the tightest ancestor, the root
        /// included. Always set, since every root is finite.
        ancestor-tokens: u64,
        /// The coarse time bound: the least time left under any ancestor's
        /// limit, each on its own clock. Always set.
        ancestor-duration: duration,
    }

    /// The caller's own place in the tree.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record lineage {
        /// The caller's session id.
        session: string,
        /// None for a root session.
        parent: option<string>,
        /// 0 for a root session.
        depth: u32,
        /// What it has left to spend.
        remaining: remaining-budget,
    }

    /// A change to the caller's subtree, from `watch`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-event {
        /// A child as it stands when the watch starts; all of these come
        /// first.
        existing(child),
        /// The snapshot is complete.
        synced,
        /// A child appeared, or its state, failure, usage or ended flag
        /// changed. Carries the whole record. Usage-only changes are
        /// coalesced to at most one per child per second.
        changed(child),
        /// A child left the listing (past the retention of ended children).
        removed(string),
        /// An event not captured above. Safe to ignore.
        other(string),
    }

    /// How a run the caller started in a child ended. Host-authored.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant run-end {
        /// It finished with an outcome.
        done,
        /// It was cancelled.
        cancelled,
        /// It failed.
        failed,
        /// Its lease expired.
        host-lost,
        /// An ending not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// A run the caller started in a child has ended.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record notice {
        /// Its place in the caller's notice sequence, increasing by one.
        seq: u64,
        /// The child's session id.
        session: string,
        /// The run's id, as `wait` takes it.
        run: string,
        /// How it ended.
        ended: run-end,
    }

    /// Why an operation on a child did nothing.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant child-error {
        /// No session with that id is a direct child of the caller.
        unknown,
        /// The child has ended (only operations that need a live child
        /// answer this).
        ended,
        /// The session store could not be reached or could not answer
        /// consistently, or a host limit (such as concurrent watches,
        /// recommended four per caller) is reached. Retry may succeed.
        unavailable(string),
        /// A failure not captured above. Diagnostic detail; never parse it.
        other(string),
    }

    /// Why `wait` returned no outcome.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    variant wait-error {
        /// The child is unknown, or the store could not be reached.
        child(child-error),
        /// The caller never started a run with that id in that child.
        unknown-run,
        /// The run failed, or its outcome is no longer kept
        /// (`run-error.expired`).
        run(run-error),
    }

    /// A run the caller started in a child.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record sent {
        /// The run's id, minted by the host.
        run: string,
        /// Progress, then the outcome.
        started: started-run,
    }

    /// What `read-notices` found.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record notice-batch {
        /// Unacknowledged notices, oldest first.
        notices: list<notice>,
        /// True while notices the caller never acknowledged have been
        /// dropped past retention; it stays true until the caller
        /// acknowledges past the gap.
        gap: bool,
    }

    /// What `spawn` started.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    record spawned {
        /// The child's session id.
        session: string,
        /// The first run, when `input` was given.
        run: option<sent>,
    }

    /// Admit and start a child session, and its first run when `input` is
    /// given; resolves once accepted. Ticket consumption, the child's
    /// creation and the first run's reservation are one atomic step.
    ///
    /// `key` is the caller's idempotency key: 1 to 128 bytes, unique per
    /// intended child in the caller's session. A resend with a key that
    /// admitted a child returns that child and the first run's `sent` (a
    /// fresh view of the same run) and starts nothing, for the life of the
    /// caller's session. A different key is a different child. When
    /// requests with different keys share a pending approval, its grant
    /// admits the first key to resend; the others get a new approval marked
    /// as a repeat.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    spawn: async func(
        request: spawn-request,
        input: option<user-message>,
        key: string,
        trace-parent: option<string>,
    ) -> result<spawned, admission-error>;

    /// Start a run in a direct child; resolves when accepted. The host
    /// re-checks the child's admission first (`denied`, with failure
    /// `revoked`, when it no longer fits). `busy` while a run is in progress
    /// there or a cancelled one has not stopped.
    ///
    /// `key` is an optional idempotency key, checked right after the caller
    /// is confirmed as the child's spawner and before every other check
    /// (busy, ended, budgets, admission): a resend with a key that already
    /// started a run in that child returns the same run (a fresh view) and
    /// starts nothing, even if the run has ended; with a different message it
    /// is `invalid-input`. The
    /// host keeps the keys of the 256 most recent runs per child; an older
    /// key starts a new run. A known key whose outcome is gone returns the
    /// run with outcome `run-error.expired`.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    send: async func(
        session: string,
        message: user-message,
        key: option<string>,
        trace-parent: option<string>,
    ) -> result<sent, start-error>;

    /// The outcome of a run the caller started in a direct child, once it
    /// ends. Works from any later run and any host sharing the store, and
    /// for an ended child while it stays listed. The host keeps at least the
    /// 16 most recent outcomes per child.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    wait: async func(session: string, run: string) -> result<run-outcome, wait-error>;

    /// Stop the runs in a direct child's subtree, as Stopping describes.
    /// Each ends `cancelled`, or `host-lost` if its host was unreachable.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    cancel: async func(session: string) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// Stop the subtree's runs as `cancel` does, and end the child and every
    /// session below it.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    end: async func(session: string) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// The caller's children, oldest first; with `descendants`, its whole
    /// subtree, each session after its parent.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    children: async func(descendants: bool) -> result<list<child>, child-error>;

    /// The same listing, then each change. A slow reader may miss an
    /// intermediate change but always sees each child's latest record. Ends
    /// with the caller's session; dropping it stops watching.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    watch: async func(descendants: bool) -> result<stream<child-event>, child-error>;

    /// Unacknowledged notices of the caller's child runs that ended. Reading
    /// clears nothing, so a lost reply is read again. The host keeps at
    /// least the 256 most recent per caller. Read at the start of each run.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    read-notices: async func() -> result<notice-batch, child-error>;

    /// Acknowledge notices through `through`; a value past the newest
    /// issued `seq` acknowledges only up to it. Repeating is harmless.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    ack-notices: async func(through: u64) -> result<_, child-error>;

    /// The caller's own session id, parent, depth and remaining budget.
    @unstable(feature = agent-spawn)
    get-lineage: async func() -> result<lineage, child-error>;
}
